Keep self in a Lua callback 中文 | English Run this command at the repository root to replay the examples through the local Unity MCP. The command also captures screenshots in both languages. python Docs/Manual/manual/tools/tutorials.py run lua-callbacks-and-self --capture Click to expand MCP actionsClick to collapse MCP actions Prepare the teaching sample. The recipe provides ${variables}. { "variables": { "script": "${fixture}/InputTutorial.lua", "prefab": "${fixture}/Tutorial_LuaCallbacks.prefab" }, "calls": [ { "tool": "invoke", "arguments": { "path": "static:UnityEditor.AssetDatabase/CreateFolder", "args": [ "${fixtureParent}", "${folder}" ] }, "expect": { "regex": "\(String\) [a-f0-9]{32}" } } ] } The engine calls a callback when an event occurs. To let a handler access the current object, capture the instance in a closure and call its method with a colon inside that closure. 1. Register the key callbacks Save this example as InputTutorial.lua and attach it with LuaBehaviorMono. The example binds W and uses the action name ManualInputTest on one object. Give each object a unique action name if several objects need a binding. Behavior() Property = { keycode = { type = "string", value = "w" } } local InputTutorial = class("InputTutorial") function InputTutorial:OnStarted() InputAPI.RegisterKeyInput("ManualInputTest", self.keycode, function(context) self:OnKeyPerformed(context) end, function(context) self:OnKeyCanceled(context) end) end function InputTutorial:OnKeyPerformed(context) print("pressed") end function InputTutorial:OnKeyCanceled(context) print("released") end function InputTutorial:OnDestroyed() InputAPI.UnregisterKeyInput("ManualInputTest") end return InputTutorial The two functions passed to RegisterKeyInput retain the current self. When the object is destroyed, pass the same action name to UnregisterKeyInput. Click to expand MCP actionsClick to collapse MCP actions [ { "tool": "invoke", "arguments": { "path": "static:System.IO.File/WriteAllText", "args": [ "${script}", "Behavior()
Property = { keycode = { type = "string", value = "w" } }

local InputTutorial = class("InputTutorial")

function InputTutorial:OnStarted()
 InputAPI.RegisterKeyInput("ManualInputTest", self.keycode,
 function(context) self:OnKeyPerformed(context) end,
 function(context) self:OnKeyCanceled(context) end)
end

function InputTutorial:OnKeyPerformed(context)
 print("pressed")
end

function InputTutorial:OnKeyCanceled(context)
 print("released")
end

function InputTutorial:OnDestroyed()
 InputAPI.UnregisterKeyInput("ManualInputTest")
end

return InputTutorial
